item.content:encoded:
In the first part, we wired up Hotwire in a Marten application and built a small task list. Clicking "Create task" loaded a form into a Turbo Frame. Pretty nice for something that barely involved JavaScript!But a task app where you can't complete a task feels a little broken. Nothing is done or is everything already done? Or maybe I just forgot to add some mechanisms for that 🤷🏻.Let's fix that with Turbo Streams. New tasks should appear in the list without a full page reload, completing a task should update just that item, and deleting one should remove it.Turbo, Stimulus, and importmap are already in place from the first part, so we can focus on the behavior this time. We'll start with task creation.Before adding the streams, let's extract the markup for a task into src/templates/tasks/_task.html:{{ task.title }}The  tag comes from marten-turbo. For a task with ID 42, it produces . We'll use that ID later to update or remove exactly this element.In , replace the existing list with:No tasks yet.
  {% for task in records %}
    {% include "tasks/_task.html" %}
  {% endfor %}
The surrounding "Create task" link and empty  frame stay as they were. The list now has an ID too, so we can append things to it.One small detail: the empty message is always in the HTML. A little CSS handles its visibility:When a task appears, the message hides. When we delete the last task, it comes back. Conveniently, our stream responses don't have to do anything about it. Modern CSS is awesome!Also add  to . We're going to append new tasks at the bottom, so the initial page should use the same order. Otherwise a reload could rearrange our list. Small but important!


  Creating a task with Turbo Streams
Our create handler already knows how to validate and save a task. We can keep that and switch its parent class to the Turbo-aware version: tells the handler which template to render after a successful Turbo submission.  makes the created record available as , matching the variable our partial already uses.Now create src/templates/tasks/create.turbo_stream.html:{% turbo_stream 'append' 'tasks' template: 'tasks/_task.html' %}
{% turbo_stream 'replace' 'new_task' do %}{% end_turbo_stream %}
That's the entire response template.After saving, the task appears in the list and the form closes.Why replace the frame instead of removing it? Our "Create task" link still needs that frame as its target. Replacing it also clears the  attribute Turbo added when it loaded the form, so clicking the link again can fetch a fresh form.A successful submission is the easy part. Let's submit an empty title.We want the form to stay open and show the error from Marten. For that, move the frame and form into src/templates/tasks/_form.html:
    {% csrf_input %}
    Title:
    {% for error in schema.title.errors %}
      {{ error.message }}
    {% endfor %}
    CreateThen replace the frame in  with {% include "tasks/_form.html" %}. Keep the page's template inheritance and content block around it.I've removed the input's  attribute here so we can actually send an empty title to Marten and see its validation response. The schema still validates the title on the server:The old  field is gone from this schema. Creating a task only needs a title; completion will get its own schema in a moment.Add one more setting to :And create that template:{% turbo_stream 'replace' 'new_task' template: 'tasks/_form.html' %}
The handler returns a 422 response for invalid submissions and renders the form with Marten's validation errors. The form stays open so we can try again.


  Finally, completing some work
Let's add a checkbox. First, a small schema in src/schemas/task_completion_schema.cr:And an update handler in src/handlers/tasks/complete_handler.cr:Add its route inside :The handler only accepts POST requests. We don't need a separate page for editing one checkbox.Its stream template, tasks/complete.turbo_stream.html, is another one-liner:{% turbo_stream 'replace' task template: 'tasks/_task.html' %}
This time we pass the record itself as the target. The tag derives its DOM ID, so  becomes something like , matching the  from our partial.
    {% csrf_input %}
    Save{{ task.title }}And give completed tasks a little styling:Check the box and click Save. The task is saved and shown as completed.There's a slightly sneaky detail in the form: the hidden input before the checkbox. An unchecked checkbox sends no value at all. We need an explicit  to clear an existing completion value. When checked, both values are sent, and Marten takes the last one: .If you've used Rails form helpers, this probably looks familiar.


  A little Stimulus, finally
The Save button works, but clicking it after every checkbox change feels unnecessary. This is a good job for Stimulus.marten stimulus generate controller autosubmit
Replace its generated contents with:Then add the controller and action to the completion form:The checkbox's  event bubbles up to the form, where Stimulus calls . We can put the Save button inside  so it's only displayed when JavaScript is disabled:SaveOur importmap and controller loading were already configured in the first part. Rebuild and restart the server after adding the controller so its pin appears in the generated importmap.Now clicking the checkbox submits the form, saves the task, and replaces the list item. Stimulus also connects the controller on the replacement form, so the next click works too.And that's all the custom JavaScript for this feature. The controller doesn't even know what a task is. It just submits its form.We can create and complete tasks now. Let's also add a way to delete them.Create src/handlers/tasks/delete_handler.cr:The handler also lets us return a  directly from Crystal. For deletion, that saves us a template file.And add a second form after the title's  inside :
  {% csrf_input %}
  DeleteClick Delete, the record is deleted, and the stream removes its list item. If that was the last task, our empty message becomes visible again through CSS.We now have a task list where creating, completing, and deleting tasks updates just the relevant elements on the page.What I like about the Marten integration is how little we had to add to our existing handlers. We switched to the Turbo-aware base classes, configured the stream templates, and reused the same task partial throughout. The only custom JavaScript is our small autosubmit controller, but it feels SPA like nonetheless!
